  <!--
    Module-skeleton convention (spec §7.6). Blast-radius lists below denote
    CONTENT OWNERSHIP — which plan fills a file's body — not file creation.
    Cycle 6.1 (foundation) additionally SCAFFOLDS, as no-op stubs, files it
    does not own the content of:
      src/server/tools/{corpus,roots,snapshot,ingest,pdf,papers,digest,
                        prompts,annotations,query,backup,inspect}.ts   (filled by their owners)
      src/server/db/raw-ddl.ts                    (filled by extraction)
    foundation's stub creation is a wave-0 act, strictly ordered before any
    fill, so no two plans ever modify the same file's content. The only
    concurrent wave (wave 2: ingest, extraction, annotations) is
    content-disjoint. worktree="not-required" is therefore sound — and the
    pinned cross-plan contracts in spec §7.6 (ServerContext, PdfChild,
    ConfigAccessor, registerTools, runRawDdl) guarantee no downstream plan
    edits a foundation-owned file.

    Dependency-pre-declaration invariant (spec §6.1, S4-pinned). No
    downstream plan runs `bun add` or edits `package.json`, `bun.lock`, or
    `tsconfig.json`. Foundation pre-declares the full v1 runtime + dev npm
    dep set at cycle 6.1; the enumerated list lives in spec §6.1's
    "Pre-declaration of dependencies" paragraph. Wave-2 worktrees therefore
    cannot collide on dep edits, which is what makes worktree="not-required"
    sound for the concurrent wave. Any later dep change is a foundation-only
    edit, not a per-plan one.

    View-opener → owning stub mapping (spec §7.6, S1-pinned). Each of the
    five view-opener tools (scholar.dashboard, scholar.paper.show,
    scholar.digest.show, scholar.prompts.show, scholar.progress.show) is
    registered from inside a specific foundation-scaffolded stub — see the
    table in spec §7.6. plan-md authors must consult that table when
    assigning registerTool calls to a plan; e.g., scholar.dashboard lives in
    corpus.ts (owned by the corpus plan), scholar.progress.show lives in
    papers.ts (owned by the extraction plan, which also owns
    scholar.paper.show / scholar.digest.show / scholar.prompts.show).

    Plan-internal cycle ordering. Within a plan, cycles execute in the
    numeric order shown in the cycles attribute. The load-bearing case is
    extraction: cycle 6.5 fills runRawDdl in raw-ddl.ts to materialize
    chunk_vec, then cycle 6.6 extends the same file with the reading_queue
    view. The cycles must land in 6.5 → 6.6 order so the cycle 6.6 test that
    queries reading_queue runs against the chunk_vec already created in 6.5.

    plan-md lifecycle. Each <plan> below has plan-md="" because the
    spec-pipeline:spec-to-multi-plan workflow populates that attribute (with
    docs/superpowers/plans/<plan-id>.md) when it authors the per-plan
    plan-md. plan-exec dispatch will refuse to run a plan with an empty
    plan-md attribute; ingest-spec is responsible for the fill-in.

    Foundation file carve-outs inside sibling directory grants
    (added 2026-05-24 by spec-to-multi-plan lead in response to a
    foundation teammate drift report). Two files live INSIDE directories
    nominally granted to sibling plans but are SPEC-OWNED BY FOUNDATION:

      src/server/ingest/primitives.ts   (foundation owns; ingest imports)
                                        spec §6.1 + §12.0
      src/server/ollama/client.ts       (foundation owns; extraction imports)
                                        spec §7.6 (singleton, not on ctx)

    Both paths appear EXPLICITLY in foundation's blast-radius below. The
    ingest plan's `src/server/ingest/` grant and the extraction plan's
    `src/server/ollama/` grant are read as "everything in that directory
    EXCEPT the files explicitly listed in another plan's blast-radius."
    blast-radius="..." is an explicit path list (no glob, no minus
    operator), so the carve-out is by convention, not by syntax — this
    comment is the canonical statement. Foundation scaffolds both files
    as content stubs at cycle 6.1 (joining the wave-0 stub set), and
    cycle 6.5+ extraction / cycle 6.4 ingest fill in their consumers
    without touching the stub files.
  -->
